Don't treat a PipeWire error on one object as a lost connection.

PwCore::onError() treats every error event except ENOENT as fatal, and
PwConnection::onFatalError() then resets the defaults and the whole registry
and reconnects. PipeWire also sends error events for single objects: when a
node enters PW_NODE_STATE_ERROR, impl-node.c calls pw_resource_error() on
every client resource bound to that node, and the client receives it as a
core error whose id is that node's proxy, not PW_ID_CORE. A lost connection
is reported on PW_ID_CORE (module-protocol-native.c, "connection error").

So one node failing (a Bluetooth transport, "code -14 Received error event",
or the Apple Silicon speaker DSP refusing a rate change, "code -1 error
changing node state: Operation not permitted") tears down every node, link
and device the shell shows, and that teardown is where it segfaults.
pipewire_bind_after_registry_reset.patch (#1261) makes the teardown safe;
this patch keeps a single node's error from causing one.

https://github.com/omacom/omarchy/issues/15838

diff --git a/src/services/pipewire/core.cpp b/src/services/pipewire/core.cpp
index 5077abe..a4ab360 100644
--- a/src/services/pipewire/core.cpp
+++ b/src/services/pipewire/core.cpp
@@ -152,7 +152,16 @@ void PwCore::onError(void* data, quint32 id, qint32 /*seq*/, qint32 res, const c
 		return;
 	}
 
-	qCWarning(logLoop) << "Pipewire error on object" << id << "with code" << res << message;
+	// Only errors on the core object itself concern the connection; the protocol reports a
+	// lost connection that way. An error on any other id belongs to that one object, such
+	// as a node that failed to change state, and the connection is still fine. Tearing the
+	// whole registry down for it destroys every node and link the shell is showing.
+	if (id != PW_ID_CORE) {
+		qCWarning(logLoop) << "Pipewire error on object" << id << "with code" << res << message;
+		return;
+	}
+
+	qCWarning(logLoop) << "Pipewire core error with code" << res << message;
 
 	emit self->fatalError();
 }
